    ],
    "notes": "in-range offer ids (int64 in XDR, 0 to 2^63-1 because core refuses negative ids from protocol 15). accepted cases carry a ManageSellOfferOp with that offer id, in forms that name exactly one id but are not its canonical spelling, so a decoder may accept them or refuse them. (1) decimal text with leading zeros (016, 00): js-stellar-sdk 17.3.0, the release that fixed js-stellar-sdk#1785, deliberately accepts these and its JSDoc for the muxed and offer id parameters says so, because they encode the id the caller wrote. (2) integral JSON numbers written with a zero fraction (16.0), which RFC 8259 treats as the same number. (3) JSON numbers above 2^53-1: not a network rule but a JSON interoperability limit, since RFC 8259 section 6 says integers outside [-(2^53)+1, 2^53-1] are not interoperable when implementations hold numbers as IEEE-754 doubles; js-stellar-sdk#1785 is that failure, where such a number lost precision and a different id was encoded. each case expects either the exact id or a refusal (leading-zero, integral-fraction or unsafe-integer); encoding any other id fails.",
